%S A400593 1,7,23,47,79,118,166,167,223,287,359,439,526,622,724,723,619,523,617,
%T A400593 719,829,947,827,717,613,614,718,830,831,951,1077,1213,1214,1356,1355,
%U A400593 1354,1506,1507,1667,1509,1669,1511,1361,1217,1082,956,838,839,727,623
%N A400593 Squares visited by a chess king moving on the square spiral, always moving to an adjacent unvisited square whose number has the greatest prime factor; ties are resolved by choosing the lowest number.
%C A400593 The board is numbered by the standard square spiral, with 1 at the origin, 2 immediately to its right, and 3 above 2. The king starts at square 1. At each step it moves to one of the eight adjacent squares that has not previously been visited.
%C A400593 The sequence is finite. The last term is a(256) = 9747.
%H A400593 Bartlomiej Pawlik, <a href="/A400593/b400593.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..256</a>
%e A400593 a(2) = 7. The squares adjacent to 1 are numbered 2 through 9. Their greatest prime factors are 2, 3, 2, 5, 3, 7, 2, 3, respectively, so square 7 is chosen.
%e A400593 From square 7, the available neighboring squares include 6, 8, 19, 20, 21, 22 and 23. Their greatest prime factors are 3, 2, 19, 5, 7, 11 and 23, respectively, so a(3) = 23.
%t A400593 Module[{u,m=DeleteCases[Tuples[{-1,0,1},2],{0,0}],g},u[{x_,y_}]:=1+Which[y>=Abs[x],4y^2-y-x,-x>=Abs[y],4x^2-x-y,-y>=Abs[x],(4y-3)y+x,True,(4x-3)x+y];g[n_]:=Max[First/@FactorInteger[n]];#[[2,-1]]&/@NestList[Function[s,With[{c=Select[{u[#],#}&/@((s[[1]]+#)&/@m),!MemberQ[s[[2]],#[[1]]]&]},If[c=={},s,With[{z=First@SortBy[c,{-g[#[[1]]]&,#[[1]]&}]},{z[[2]],Append[s[[2]],z[[1]]]}]]]],{{0,0},{1}},255]]
%Y A400593 Cf. A006530, A335816, A333713, A335856, A335661.
%K A400593 nonn,walk,fini,full,new
